diff --git a/packages/coding-agent/CHANGELOG.md b/packages/coding-agent/CHANGELOG.md
index 9de6ab2ad3..b6e315312b 100644
--- a/packages/coding-agent/CHANGELOG.md
+++ b/packages/coding-agent/CHANGELOG.md
@@ -7,7 +7,7 @@
 - When a background subagent resolves its model, the spawning agent now gets a hidden message naming it, such as ``Subagent `fable` started on `provider/model` (thinking level low).`` It is sent again if the subagent moves to another model. Before this the agent had only the name it gave the worker and could take it for a different model.
 - Added the `retry.accountMode` setting (`sequential` by default, or `balanced`). With `sequential`, Anthropic accounts are used one at a time: every session and subagent follows the account that served last until it is blocked or spent, then moves to the next.
 - New `usage_bar` status-line segment, in the `default` preset right after the context percent: a five-cell progress bar, the percent used and the window tag (`━━━━━ 100% 7d`) for the active account. New `statusLine.usageWindow` setting (`weekly` by default, `session`, `worst`) chooses the 7 Day window, the 5 Hour window or the more used one. For a Claude model with its own weekly counter (Fable, Mythos, Opus, Sonnet) `weekly` uses the more used of the shared and the model's 7 Day row, tagged `7d fable`; a provider without a 7 Day window draws no bar in `weekly` mode, and `session` falls back to its shortest window under its own tag. It renders nothing when the provider reports no usage windows, or when several accounts have no resolved one.
-- When a provider holds two or more stored OAuth accounts, the status line names the session's account in the model: `Anthropic (B) > Sonnet 5.5`, and the footer's raw model id reads `anthropic-b/claude-sonnet-5-5`. The letter is the account's position in `omp usage accounts` order (A first) and follows an account change mid-session. Single-account providers look as before.
+- When a provider holds two or more stored OAuth accounts, the status line names the session's account in the model by a short form of its email: `Anthropic (drewett.ki…) > Sonnet 5.5`, and the footer's raw model id reads `anthropic-drewett.ki…/claude-sonnet-5-5`. The tag is the part before the `@`, cut to ten characters. It grows when two accounts would show the same text, and follows an account change mid-session. Single-account providers look as before.
 - Running background tasks now have their own pinned shelf directly above the Subagents shelf, in the same style: backgrounded `bash` commands, backgrounded `eval` cells and services, each as a row with its id (such as `bg_1099`), a one-line preview of the command and its elapsed time. Jobs that run a subagent stay in the Subagents shelf. `display.pinnedJobs` (`collapsed` by default, `full`, `off`) sets how many rows show before an expander, as `display.pinnedAgents` does for subagents.
 - Name an operator-configured model role such as `@worker` in a task item's `model`, in a mixed-model batch if you like; it resolves to that role's current model and goes through the same `task.perCallModel` gate and approval as an exact `provider/model-id`.
 - Task subagents called without `model` now get a configured model role chosen by the native judge. When the judge fails, times out, is unsure or is unavailable, no model is chosen and the reason is reported; there is no fallback role. Task results show the requested and resolved model and a `modelRoute` note such as `Automatic model selection: @sonnet.`
@@ -22,7 +22,7 @@
 
 ### Changed
 
-- A subagent never takes the parent's model, the session model or the `default` role, and it runs on exactly one model for its whole life. Every spawn without a per-call, host or seance model, from the task tool or from eval `agent()`/`workpool()`, goes through automatic model selection. One usable role alias is chosen without the judge (`only one configured alias`); two or more go to the judge, and a judge that fails, times out or is not confident chooses nothing (there is no fallback role). When selection cannot choose, a `task.agentModelOverrides` entry or the agent's own `model` is used with a visible warning (a notice, a warning line on the subagent row and a `<model-warning>` in the result); with neither, the spawn fails before the child starts and the error tells the parent agent to stop and report to you instead of changing settings.
+- A subagent never takes the parent's model, the session model or the `default` role, and it runs on exactly one model for its whole life. Every spawn without a per-call, host or seance model, from the task tool or from eval `agent()`/`workpool()`, goes through automatic model selection. One usable role alias is chosen without the judge (`only one configured alias`); two or more go to the judge, and a judge that fails, times out or is not confident chooses nothing (there is no fallback role). When selection cannot choose, the spawn fails before the child starts and the error tells the parent agent to stop and report to you instead of changing settings. Neither a `task.agentModelOverrides` entry nor the agent's own `model` takes over.
 - A subagent no longer falls back to the parent's model when its own model has no working credentials, and it no longer inherits the `default` or a role fallback chain. Only a chain you wrote in `retry.fallbackChains` under the exact `provider/model-id` it runs on can move it, and using it raises the same warning. A task result whose child ended on another model than it started on says so in a `<model-warning>`.
 - `task.prewalk`, `task.agentPrewalk` and an agent's `prewalk` frontmatter no longer apply to subagents: a subagent never hands off to another model. Session-level prewalk is unchanged.
 - `omp update` and the startup update check now use the latest stable release of the fork's GitHub repository (`kierandrewett/omp`, versions like `18.14.2-fork.1`): the `omp-linux-x64` asset is checked against `SHA256SUMS.txt` before it replaces the running binary, and the previous binary is restored if the new one fails version verification. `PI_BUILD_URL` and the upstream npm registry are no longer consulted, and npm, bun, Homebrew, mise and Nix installs are not updated from here.
